|> Exercise
    handle=_eta_approximation_from_cosine_graph_
    The function below is also the blue
    curve from Exercise >>_four_particles_, known as the
    _cosine function_ (already encountered
    in Chapter >>functions, Exercise >>_cosine_of_1000x_).
    Knowing that this function is the $x$-coordinate of a
    point rotating at unit speed around a unit
    circle what is an approximation to the
    circumference of a unit circle that can be
    deduced by looking (closely) at the graph?

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_cosine_for_eta.svg

    |> Solution

        Because the particle is going at unit speed the
        circumference of the unit circle is equal to the
        amount of time it takes the particle to complete one
        revolution of the circle—that is, for example, the
        time covered by the yellow interval below, from crest
        to crest*:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_cosine_for_eta_with_yellow.svg

        (*We're just going to assume that the graph
        passes through the point $(0, 1)$, which is as good
        as our eyesight can tell, anyway.)
        From the graph, then, it seems as if the circumference
        of a unit circle is

        $$
        {\Large \approx 6.3}
        $$

        and another possibility would be

        $$
        \Large \approx {12.5\over 2} = 6.25
        $$

        since it seems like the graph ends at $t = 12.5$ (?)
        after going through exactly (??) two cycles.

        However, the exact position of “cresting” is hard to
        determine because the graph flattens at that point,
        and a more clever clue to pick up on is actually that the graph seems to go almost exactly through
        $(t, x) = (11, 0)$:
        !! at which point it is jogging straight up:
        !! or thereabouts. However, but a bit of detective work
        !! reveals a more precise approximation: note 
        !! that the graph seems to go almost exactly through
        !! the point $(11, 0)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_cosine_for_eta_11_noticed.svg

        This implies that in
        $$
        {\Large 11}
        $$
        units of time—or nearly so—the particle does
        $$
        {\Large 7}
        $$
        quarter-turns,
        as each half-bump corresponds to a
        quarter-turn, if you think about it, and there
        are seven half-bumps from $t = 0$ to that point:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_cosine_for_eta_counting_quarter_turns.svg

        Thus

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \Large {4\over 7} \cdot 11 = {44 \over 7} = 6.\overline{285714}
            $$

        !! Thus

        !! $$
        !! \Large {11 \over 7}
        !! $$

        !! units of time is approximately one quarter-turn, and

        !! $$
        !! \Large 4 \cdot {11 \over 7} = {44 \over 7}
        !! $$

        !! units of time is approximately one full
        !! revolution (four quarter-turns), i.e.,

        !! |> Boxed
        !!     $$
        !!     \Large {44 \over 7} = 6.\overline{285714}
        !!     $$
        
        must be an approximation to the circumference of a
        unit circle, by the graph. 
        !! that we can guess from the graph. 
        !! (That
        !! corroborates at least somewhat with our initial
        !! approximations of $6.3$ and $6.25$.)
        !! (But that is
        !! _not_ exact, because the graph does _not_ actually
        !! quite go through the point $(11, 0)$.) (Though it
        !! _does_ go through the point $(0, 1)$, as cosine is
        !! defined.)
        
        !! which ends
        !! up being about half-a-part-in-a-thousand too
        !! large ($0.040249943...\%$ too large) (or just:
        !! “$0.00040249943...$ too large”) whereas our initial
        !! approximations of

        !! $$
        !! \Large 6.3
        !! $$

        !! and

        !! $$
        !! \Large 6.25
        !! $$

        !! ends up being roughly half-a-part-in-one-hundred
        !! too large, $10$ times worse!

        |> SolutionNote

            This approximation ends up being about half-a-part-in-a-thousand
            too large, or

            $$
            0.040249943...\%
            $$
            
            too large (or just: “$0.00040249943...$ too large”, no “$\%$”
            needed if you shift the decimals over two places), because
            the graph is actually crossing through $0$ just shy of $t = 11$,
            not _at_ $t = 11$.

        |> SolutionNote

            The “core” approximation is actually that of

            $$
            \Large {11\over 7}
            $$

            for one-quarter the circumference of a unit circle; from
            which,

            !! $$
            !! \Large {22\over 7}, {33\over 7}, \,\,\te{and}\,\, {44\over 7}
            !! $$
            $$
            \Large {22\over 7}, {33\over 7},
            $$

            and

            $$
            \Large {44\over 7}
            $$
            are derived approximations for one-half, three-quarters,
            and the full circumference of the unit circle!